    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Upwelling in the equatorial Pacific changes in intensity according to the ENSO (El Niño Southern Oscillation) phase. The cold phase of the cycle (La Niña) is associated with intense upwelling that brings cold and oxygen-depleted water closer to the surface. The warm phase with reduced upwelling, El Niño, deepens the mixed layer and brings warmer waters closer to the surface. In this thesis, I hypothesize that low oxygen concentration and temperature associated with La Niña conditions form an oceanographic boundary that precludes blue marlin migrations due to limitations of their circulatory and cardiac physiology. The purpose of this study is to determine how ENSO events in the equatorial Pacific Ocean affect the long-range movements and depth distributions of blue marlin (Makaira nigricans). I use pop-up satellite telemetry data from tag deployments in 2009-2012, during which a moderate El Niño, a strong La Niña, a weak La Niña, and a normal year occurred. To examine the impacts of ENSO variability on blue marlin movements I compare World Ocean Atlas and shipboard in situ data sets in the geographic region from 30oN-20oS and 175oW-115oW to analyze diving behavior in relation to physical oceanographic conditions during this period. For blue marlin tagged in this region, temperature and depth distributions revealed a preference for a warmer subset (>24oC) of the available thermal habitat and a bimodal diving behavior consistent with previous studies of this species. Blue marlin spent 85.25% of their time in surface waters warmer than 25.5oC and 14.75% in those less than 25.5oC. The ENSO phase and related changes in sea surface temperatures and upwelling strength affected not only the probability of trans-equatorial blue marlin migrations, but also their longitudinal location along the equator. Blue marlin traversed regions of cold surface water coupled with downwelling, warm surface water combined with upwelling, but not cold surface water in concert with upwelling. These results suggest that sea surface temperature is the limiting factor for blue marlin migrations across upwelling regions of the equatorial Pacific south of Hawaii. These findings will inform future efforts to conserve blue marlin populations and their ability to exchange genetic information across oceanographic boundaries.",
